Claude Code Brief — Print Studio: School Mode (Middle & High School)
Context
CultureSchool is preparing Print Studio for a middle/high-school pilot via a principal contact in Florida, targeting a spring-semester unit. Demo-able school mode is needed by early November (teachers lock spring plans Nov–Dec). Business model: per-school annual site license; pilot school free year one.

This build depends on / extends the asset-library audit+spec (docs/asset-library-audit.md, docs/asset-library-spec.md). Read those first. The school_safe rights gate specced there becomes load-bearing here.

Regulatory frame (drives every decision below): FERPA applies to student records; COPPA applies to under-13s — 6th graders are often 12, so middle school means COPPA-grade caution. The safest architecture is collect no student PII at all. Florida F.S. 1003.42 and the CPALMS Visual Arts standards shape the content side (see docs/florida-alignment-brief if imported; benchmarks VA.68.* verified, VA.912.* to be added).
Phase 1 — CAPABILITY REVIEW (read-only, do this first)
Your first instruction: before reading the spec direction below, review what Print Studio can currently do and report it. You know this codebase better than anyone in this conversation. Everything in Phase 2 is direction to be validated against actual capability — where the code already does something close, say so and spec the smaller delta; where a direction fights the architecture, propose the version that doesn't.

Produce docs/school-mode-audit.md:

Feature capability inventory. What can a user actually DO in Print Studio today: creation tools, moodboards, pattern/palette application, text, layers, templates; save/versioning (is any version history kept?); export formats and flows; anything resembling assignments, collections, or galleries. This section drives what "school mode" and "Interpretation Briefs" can realistically be by November.
Auth & identity today. How Print Studio identifies users (email in localStorage? Supabase auth?). Every place a user identifier is written or read. This determines how invasive the class-code layer is.
PII inventory. Every field collected anywhere in the Print Studio flow (signup, save, export, share). Flag everything that would be student PII in a classroom.
Community/social surface. Any path from Print Studio into public or social features (walls, boards, profiles, sharing, external links). School mode must be able to sever all of them.
Asset pipeline. Confirm from the asset audit which sources can be filtered to school_safe (owned patterns + CC0 museum) and where the filter would apply.
Save/export model. Where creations are stored, whether they're publicly readable, how the watermark applies.
Phase 2 — SPEC + BUILD PLAN
Produce docs/school-mode-spec.md with effort estimates (S/M/L) and a build order that yields a demo by early November. Target architecture — validate against Phase 1 findings:

Class-code access, zero student PII.
Teacher creates a class → gets a 6-char class code.
Students enter class code + display name (first name + last initial or alias — teacher's choice per class). No email, no password, no account.
Session = class_id + display_name + device token. Rejoining uses the same code + name.
Tables: school_orgs, classes (teacher_email, code, name, display_name_policy), class_members (class_id, display_name, device_token), class_works (class_id, member_id, artifact ref, created_at).
Teacher dashboard. Create/rename classes, see the class gallery of student work, feature/hide pieces, export a work or the whole gallery to PDF (for printing/display), reset a student's session. Teacher auth can use the existing email pattern — teachers aren't PII-restricted the way students are.
School content gate. In school mode the asset picker shows ONLY school_safe content (owned patterns + CC0 museum assets). No uploads from student sessions in v1 (removes moderation burden). Attribution/ watermark behavior unchanged — the provenance credit line on exports is a feature here, not a constraint.
Social severance. School sessions have no wall posting, no public boards, no external share links, no profile creation. Work is visible only within the class and to the teacher.
Atlas-lite panels. Each pattern in the school picker shows its tradition card (name, origin paragraph, credit) inline — this is the curriculum surface. Content comes only from verified Atlas entries; spec the flag/join that enforces this.
Licensing/entitlement. Simplest viable: school_orgs.license_status (pilot | active | expired) checked at class creation. No payment integration in v1 — invoicing is manual at this scale.
Mode isolation. School mode should be a separate entry point (subdomain or /school route) sharing the engine, not a toggle inside the consumer app — reduces the risk of a consumer feature leaking into a classroom.
Interpretation Briefs — the assignment layer (core to the sale). Teachers assign structured "find and interpret" quests rather than blank canvases. A brief = title, prompt text, referenced tradition(s)/Atlas entries, standards tags, and what-to-turn-in checklist (documented sources → original print → written artist statement). Tables: briefs (class_id, title, prompt, atlas_refs, standards_tags, created_by), and class_works.brief_id. Students see the brief with an inline Atlas source panel (verified entries only — these ARE the citable sources); the artist statement is a text field attached to the work; the provenance credit on export doubles as the works-cited line. Ship 3 template briefs pre-loaded (content will be provided) plus teacher-editable copies. Validate against the capability inventory: if anything like collections/templates exists, briefs may be a thin layer over it.
Process receipts — AI-resistance evidence. Log key making events per class work: tradition/Atlas entries viewed, patterns tried, saves/ versions, session count and rough duration. Teacher gets a simple "process view" timeline per student work. First check what events/ versioning the code already captures (capability inventory) and spec the smallest instrumentation delta. Privacy: process data is class-scoped, visible only to the class teacher, never public, and tied to display name + device token only (no PII, consistent with the rest of school mode).
